@flexiarg meta/fill-meta-policy-slots
@title Fill Typed Slots to Instantiate META Policies
@sigils [🍵/内]
@keywords META, outer loop, slot, filler, policy, candidate, task, resource envelope, observation contract
@audience War Machine designers, pattern-policy constructors
@tone structural-operational
@references [meta/observe-the-meta-field structure/hinge-entry-slots cascades/declared-skeleton]
@status [status[ready] "evidence[E-outer-loop-improvement reason-bearing META policy; operator ruling 2026-10-02]"]

! conclusion:
  Treat an outer policy as a pattern cascade with typed holes, and instantiate
  it only by filling every load-bearing slot from current, source-pinned
  evidence.

  + context: A META field observation exists and the machine must construct
    candidate policies rather than rank bare task identifiers.

  + IF:
    A reusable outer-policy skeleton names the work kind, target, coarse move,
    resource envelope, observation contract, and stopping rule.

  + HOWEVER:
    A task identifier alone is not a policy, and optional prose fields allow
    an agent to narrate a rationale after the draw. Unfilled slots make G
    incomparable because predicted outcomes refer to different or absent acts.

  + THEN:
    Bind `task-kind`, `target`, `next-move`, `resource-envelope`,
    `evidence-channel`, and `stopping-rule` with typed fillers. Reject an
    instance when a required filler is absent or violates its guard. Retain the
    slot-to-source receipt as the identity of the instantiated policy.

  + BECAUSE:
    G is defined over policies with predicted consequences. Typed slot filling
    turns a reusable cascade into a concrete, auditable act whose consequences
    can be modelled and compared.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Construct at least one filled policy for each admissible arm and preserve refused fillings as typed exclusions.]
